Conduct Disorder
Mental Health & Substance Use
Aggression toward people/animals, Destruction of property, Serious violation of rules

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MHS-017
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of physical aggression resulting in a minor hand injury to the patient and significant property destruction.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parent
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an active behavioral crisis involving severe property destruction, physical aggression toward the parent, and a bleeding hand injury sustained from punching a wall. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to assess the traumatic injury, de-escalate the immediate violence, and ensure the safety of the household, which could not be safely delayed until morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "He just exploded because I took his phone. He smashed the wall, hit me, and now his hand is bleeding everywhere."
History of Present Illness: The current crisis erupted one hour ago when the parent enforced a screen-time limit. The patient reacted with explosive rage, punching a wall and shoving the parent. The parent reports a long-standing, repetitive and persistent pattern of behavior in which the basic rights of others are violated. This includes a history of aggression toward people and animals, frequent bullying, and initiation of physical fights at school. The patient has a history of destruction of property, including a minor fire-setting incident last year. There is ongoing deceitfulness, including stealing money from the parent's purse, and serious violations of rules, such as frequent truancy from school beginning before age 13 and running away from home overnight.
Functional Status: Highly disruptive; currently suspended from school.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of escalating violence toward family members; potential for legal involvement.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/75 | HR: 98 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 60 Kg
General: Sullen, hostile, avoiding eye contact, sitting with arms crossed, right hand wrapped in a bloody tissue.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular and respiratory systems unremarkable. Musculoskeletal exam of the right hand reveals a 2cm superficial laceration over the 5th metacarpophalangeal joint ("fight bite" area, though against a wall, not teeth). Moderate swelling, but full range of motion preserved; no obvious fracture on clinical exam. Mental state examination is notable for prominent hostility, lack of remorse for the damage or the assault on the parent, and clear aggression toward people/animals.
Environmental Observations: A hole is visible in the hallway drywall; a shattered lamp is on the floor.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil prescribed medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Conduct Disorder - Status: Exacerbated (Acute violent episode)
Differentials:

Oppositional Defiant Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of severe physical aggression toward others, property destruction, and serious violations of the law (theft), which exceed the criteria for ODD.

Intermittent Explosive Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely due to the calculated deceitfulness, rule violations, and proactive aggression between explosive episodes.

Attention-Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary driver; while impulsivity is present, the malicious intent and lack of remorse point to Conduct Disorder.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented following an acute episode of severe behavioral dysregulation resulting in physical trauma and property destruction. Telephone triage correctly identified the urgent need for wound assessment and behavioral containment. Urgent after-hours medical intervention was mandatory to rule out a metacarpal fracture (boxer's fracture), clean the wound to prevent infection, and assess the immediate safety of the parent. Delaying care until routine hours would have left an open wound untreated and allowed the highly volatile situation to remain unassessed, posing a continuing physical threat to the household.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Wound thoroughly irrigated, cleaned, and dressed with a simple non-adherent dressing. Advised oral ibuprofen for pain and swelling. Expected investigation results (outpatient): psychometric evaluation demonstrating callous-unemotional traits.
Positive Behavioral Change: Implement a strict "Earned Privileges" token economy. Basic needs are met, but all high-value items (phone, gaming) must be earned daily through specific, prosocial behaviors (e.g., speaking respectfully, completing one chore without argument). Reward compliance immediately. This establishes clear, predictable operant conditioning to shape behavior.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to intensive family therapy and youth behavioral intervention programs.
DME/Safety (Optional): Nil.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 48 hours to reassess the hand for possible X-ray if swelling worsens, and to initiate formal psychiatric referrals.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient becomes physically violent again, if the hand becomes red/hot/severely swollen, or if the patient expresses active suicidal/homicidal intent.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Acute wound management and de-escalation provided; severe behavioral disorders require intensive multidisciplinary intervention.
Clinical Course: The hand injury should heal with rest, but the underlying behavioral pathology is chronic and highly resistant to brief interventions.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If the hand pain becomes severe, or if you cannot move your fingers tomorrow, seek medical care. Parent: Call the police immediately if you feel your physical safety is threatened again tonight.

PATIENT EDUCATION

